  (:adr/body
  "# ADR-2607184300: Independent-memory Kotoba component linker and HTTP/TLS E2E

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-18

## Context

The transport/TLS provider was executable, but applications could receive only
native HostFunctions directly. HTTP and database `.kotoba` sources were not
yet usable as intermediate components. A first bridge attempt also proved that
reusing a consumer pointer as the same provider-memory offset corrupts provider
static data; Wasm instances do not share linear memory.

## Decision

Add a fail-closed JVM component linker. Link manifests declare the exact field,
provider export, Wasm signature, and copy-in/copy-out pointer-length pairs.
The linker grows one isolated page in provider memory, allocates every bridge
buffer within that page, rewrites provider-call pointer arguments, and copies
only declared bytes. Missing exports, duplicate bindings, invalid signatures,
negative sizes, scratch overflow, and provider output beyond consumer capacity
are rejected.

Consumer HostCaps grant `component/http` or `component/database`; provider
HostCaps separately grant the attenuated lower transport/TLS surface. A real
Kotoba HTTP consumer and real Kotoba HTTP provider are compiled during the
integration test, linked as separate instances, and execute a bounded HTTP/1.1
GET over the verified native TLS provider to a real loopback TLS server.

## Maturity boundary

The structural JVM linker gap is closed as a prototype. HTTP currently performs
one bounded response read and does not yet parse status, headers, chunking or
redirects. Browser/Node linking, concurrency stress, external PKI/DNS evidence,
production CI/soak and Q9 consumer cutover remain pending.
")))
